extraction_notes: "SPELLS ARE A NAMED LIST, NOT A LEVEL GATE. `invocations` is every spell the Nightbane invocation chapter describes, 131 names: the index on printed 126-127 for levels 1-13, plus the five level 14-15 spells the body describes and the index omits (Close Rift, Id Barrier, Restoration, Dimensional Portal, Teleport: Superior). Twelve are `Nightbane: <name>` rows, because the book''s cost differs from the shared row or the shared row is rifts-only: the six level-four spells (Astral Projection, Charismatic Aura, Cure Minor Disorders, Energy Field, Shadow Meld, Trance), Luck Curse, Metamorphosis: Human, Minor Curse, Wards, Summon and Control Rain (the index''s `Summon Rain`) and Protection Circle: Superior. Index spellings resolved to catalog rows: Fingers of Wind -> Fingers of the Wind; Negate Poisons/Toxins -> Negate Poison/Toxin; Swim as Fish at level 4 -> Swim as a Fish (lesser) and at level 5 -> Swim as a Fish (Superior); Invisibility: Superior -> Invisibility (Superior); Purification (Food/Water) -> Purification; Spoil (Water/Food) -> Spoil; Summon and Control Canine -> Summon and Control Canines; Control/Enslave Entity -> Control & Enslave Entity; Summon/Control Rodents -> Summon and Control Rodents. SUMMON ENTITY (index, level 12, 250 P.P.E.) IS LEFT OUT: the chapter prints no stat block for it and the spell import stored nothing (zzzzzzzzzzzzz-nb-spells.sql). The catalog''s `Summon and Control Entity` matches its level and cost, but this book never names it. The starting twelve are three from each of levels 1-4, one group per level. The spell gained each level is `equal to his own level`, stored as one pick from the list capped at the character''s level (BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT F61). Learning or buying further spells of any level at any time is prose. EXPERIENCE: the `Ashmedai, Psychic & Sorceror` ladder, printed 233, read off a render because the text layer interleaves the columns. It prints level 13 as 225,920-275,920 while level 12 ends at 225,920; stored as 225,921. MONEY: printed 117 gives 3D6x100+200 in cash, stored, and 3D6x1,000 in property (equipment, vehicles and similar items), which is not cash and is in restrictions. EQUIPMENT: the car or jeep is not in equipment_starting because a vehicle is not a gear row (BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT F41); the chapter''s vehicle list holds compact-nb and jeep-4-wheel-drive-nb. The chapter sells no library, magnifying glass, note pad or outfit: the library and magnifying glass are stubs, and the note pad and clothes cite the shared `note-pad` and `clothing` rows a Nightbane character already sees. The personal computer is the chapter''s Basic Computer ($650); the pocket tape recorder its Tape Recorder, which the chapter describes as pocket size. SKILLS: Read/Write/Speak Native Language is split into the speaking and the literacy rows, both at 98%. `Two Lore skills of choice` offers the six Lore skills on Nightbane''s own list (printed 48). Research is 50% in this book (printed 57), so +25% is 75. The hand to hand trade (Basic one related pick, Expert two, Martial Arts or Assassin three) cannot be charged and is a note on Physical. Secondary skills are `from those listed`, stored as the related categories without their bonuses. HIT POINTS AND S.D.C. are not printed on the entry; printed 36 gives P.E. + 1D6 per level, and 3D6 S.D.C. to everyone without a military, police, detective or athletic background."
---

## Lore

Sorcerers belong to the organised, "civilised" schools of magic that began in
ancient Egypt, Greece and Persia - the scholarly opposite of the shaman. They
believe learned knowledge is the secret of magic, and they approach wizardry the
way a scientist or scholar does: by accumulating information, and relying on
their wits and the magical energies of the world.

Modern sorcerers tend to be bookish, often found at colleges and universities
doing obscure research in history, archaeology or the occult. Beneath the nerdish
exterior there is often an adventurer''s heart. Some become self-serving
exploiters of "mere mortals", some hide their powers and defend humankind, and
most simply lose themselves in learning - but since Dark Day even the most
absent-minded knows that the ivory tower offers no protection.

Their studies have attuned them to ley lines, nexus points and other mystical
hot spots. Some know of the Nightlands and have spells to peer into them. Few
know much about the Ba''al, and they are learning the hard way. A few have met
Nightbane and taught them magic (see the Nightbane Sorcerer O.C.C.).

## GM Notes

**Spell prices**, printed 126, for a character buying spells rather than
researching them: $5,000 for level one, $10,000 for level two, $20,000 for level
three, $30,000 for level four, $40,000 for level five, $70,000 for level six,
$90,000 for level seven, $120,000 for level eight and $160,000 for level nine.
Add 10% for offensive spells and 20% for metamorphosis and summoning spells. Not
every guild offers the full range - levels 1-4 are the most common - and many
cannot teach spells at all.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'nb-sorcerer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'nb-sorcerer';
SELECT count(*) AS stub_gear FROM gear WHERE slug IN ('small-library-nb', 'magnifying-glass-nb');

